// a container returned by value can be indexed. the call result is not a place, so there is no storage
// to take the element's address in - the compiler gives it some: the container is operand 0 of the
// element call, which is an ordinary borrow argument, so it takes t_borrow_temporary and
// AST::OwnershipPass binds a temporary around the statement that reads through it (todo/A13c)
//
// **the temporary has to outlive the element call**, which is the whole reason a call binds nothing of
// its own: `operator []` hands back a `T&` *into* the array, so a bind at the call would have destroyed
// the buffer the address points into. the destructor below runs after the read, and the output order is
// the assertion

function make() : array<int32>
{
    array<int32> $a = [10, 20, 30];
    return $a;
}

echo make()[0];
echo make()[2];

// and through a nested read, where the bind is one level further out still
echo make()[1] + 5;
